      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "14d96d6e-49ba-56ef-8c91-cfc80413c070",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Magnus Barefoot, Magnus Olafsson\n- Places: Norway, British Isles, Ireland, Kingdom of the Isles, Dublin, Orkney, Shetland\n- Roles: King of Norway, rulers of the Kingdom of the Isles\n- Concepts: Suðreyjar, Southern Isles, Norðreyjar, Northern Isles, overlordship\n- Landmarks: King Magnus' Halt railway station, grave of Magnus Barefoot\n\nFacts:\n- Magnus Olafsson, better known as Magnus Barefoot, lived from 1073 to 24 August 1103.\n- Magnus Barefoot ruled as King of Norway, under the regnal name Magnus III, from 1093 until his death in 1103.\n- Magnus Barefoot's reign was marked by aggressive military campaigns and conquest in Norse-dominated parts of the British Isles and Ireland.\n- Magnus Barefoot extended his rule to the Kingdom of the Isles and Dublin.\n- The Norse called the islands of the Kingdom of the Isles the Suðreyjar, meaning the Southern Isles, in contrast to the Norðreyjar, or Northern Isles, of Orkney and Shetland.\n- The rulers of the Kingdom of the Isles were sometimes independent of external control, but for much of the period they had overlords in Norway, Ireland, England, Scotland, or Orkney.\n- King Magnus' Halt railway station was named after the nearby grave of Viking King Magnus Barefoot.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4d8333cf-e0c4-5a49-92de-211a4160488d",
        "text": "Page: Magnus Barefoot. Sentence: Magnus Olafsson (Old Norse: \"Magnús Óláfsson\", Norwegian: \"Magnus Olavsson\"; 1073 – 24 August 1103), better known as Magnus Barefoot (Old Norse: \"Magnús berfœttr\", Norwegian: \"Magnus Berrføtt\"), was King of Norway (as Magnus III) from 1093 until his death in 1103.\nPage: Magnus Barefoot. Sentence: His reign was marked by aggressive military campaigns and conquest, particularly in the Norse-dominated parts of the British Isles and Ireland, where he extended his rule to the Kingdom of the Isles and Dublin.\nPage: Kingdom of the Isles. Sentence: The islands were known to the Norse as the \"Suðreyjar\", or \"Southern Isles\" as distinct from the \"Norðreyjar\" or Northern Isles of Orkney and Shetland.\nPage: Kingdom of the Isles. Sentence: At times the rulers were independent of external control, although for much of the period they had overlords in Norway, Ireland, England, Scotland or Orkney.\nPage: King Magnus' Halt railway station. Sentence: It takes its name from the nearby grave of Viking King Magnus Barefoot.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
